\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The early morning sun cast a warm, golden glow across the kitchen as the coffee maker finished quiet, steady brewing. Soft music hummed faintly from a small counter speaker while a mom set down two warm plates of toast and sliced fruit. There was a gentle, easy rhythm to the house, built on familiar routines and early alarms that made space for quiet moments before the rest of the world woke up.\
In the next room, her eight-year-old son padded into the hallway, already dressed in his favorite navy sweater. He traded a sleepy smile for a gentle hug, slipping into his chair at the table to finish his breakfast while she checked her packed tote bag for the day\'92s university lecture notes. Everything was laid out right where it belonged\'97backpack zipped by the front door, shoes waiting by the mat, and a mug of fresh coffee sealed and ready to go.\
By twenty past seven, they stepped out into the crisp morning air, holding hands as they strolled down the quiet sidewalk toward the corner stop. The neighborhood was just starting to stir, but there was no rush, no frantic searching for lost keys, just the shared delight of pointing out a blue jay in the low branches overhead. The yellow bus pulled up right on schedule, its doors folding open with a friendly hiss.\
After a bright wave goodbye, she walked back to her sedan, enjoying the calm stillness of the drive ahead. She reached the university campus with twenty minutes to spare, grabbing her bag from the passenger seat and walking casually toward her morning class. She took her seat with a quiet sense of ease, completely ready for the day ahead.\
